Those originating in Kaṭīkadūra are considered pleasing to look at; / those from Ṛṣika cut the body, strike vital parts, and are heavy;
those from Vaṅga are sharp and withstand striking; those originating in Śūrpāraka are firm; / and those produced in Videha should be known as well-handled and powerful;
those originating in the Aṅga country and produced in the Cedi country are sharp; / those from Kāliñjara withstand weight. Likewise, I shall declare their characteristics:
Those swords are superior that have the proper measure in digits. / Having ascertained the proper measure, one should not bear one inferior to it,
nor one exceeding the measure, or one with a broken spine. / A sword whose sound, O Bhārgava, is swift and very sweet,

