Loss of harsh instruments is not desired for rulers of the earth. / Whatever task someone may have special skill in,
to that task the king should assign him after testing. / He should employ servants descended from his fathers and grandfathers in all tasks,
except in affairs concerning heirs; for there, in brief, / the king, having tested men whom he has himself advanced, in affairs concerning heirs,
should appoint them, O fortunate one; they will act for his welfare. / With the desire to win over people, those who have come from another king's court,
whether upright or wicked, he should carefully give refuge to. / Having known someone to be wicked, the lord of the earth should not place trust in him;
yet he should grant him a livelihood, with the desire to win over people. / A king should greatly honor a man who has arrived from another country.
He should consider an ally who has arrived from [his own] land with high regard. / At will, a king should not gather an excessive number of servants, O foremost of the Bhṛgus;

even when not angry, he appears enraged; even when pleased, he is fruitless; / he speaks slowly and cuts off one's livelihood;
he does not honor [the dependent] when words of admittance are spoken; / and in all acts of adoration, he behaves as if asleep;
in conversations, he reproaches with faults and interrupts speech; / and he is seen turning away when one's virtues are praised;
and when work is being done, he turns his gaze elsewhere. / Having heard the sign of one alienated, listen to the sign of an affectionate [king]:
upon seeing [him], he becomes pleased and receives his word with respect; / in inquiries about well-being, he offers a seat;
he does not suspect him in private audience and in secret; / his face becomes delighted upon hearing his conversation;
he rejoices even in unpleasant words spoken by him; / and he receives a present, even a slight one, with respect;

2.6.m:
he scratches the ground, he shakes his neck, O twice-born, / he scratches his head, and he licks his lower lip;
he acts hastily in tasks, O Rāma, even when they are perverse. / Such signs of a poisoner one should examine.
Then, acting swiftly, he should test that food in fire: / [if it has] the colour of a rainbow, accompanied by cracking like wood,
swirling in a single whorl, foul-smelling, and crackling violently— / and from inhaling its smoke a creature develops a headache.
Flies do not alight on poisoned food, O descendant of Bhṛgu; / and those that do alight perish. When poison is seen,
the gaze of the cakora bird loses its colour, O best of the Bhṛgus; / and the voice of the cuckoo becomes unnatural, O twice-born;
the gait of the swan falters, and the bhṛṅgarāja bird cries out; / the krauñca falls into excitement, and the rooster shrieks;
the cakora bird screams, and the starling shrieks; / the goldcrest turns away, and the kāraṇḍava duck perishes;

the monkey urinates, O Rāma, and the jīvañjīvaka bird faints; / the babhru mongoose's hair stands on end, and the spotted deer weeps;
and the peacock rejoices at the sight of poison, O twice-born. / Poisoned food, O Rāma, takes a long time to cook,
and it becomes excessively runny, cooked yet like stale food, / with ruined flavour and scent, and covered with spots resembling moonbeams.
Cooked dishes become dry, bubbles arise in liquids, / and a line of foam appears in salty preparations;
in meat-broth there is a blue streak, in milk a coppery one, / and in curds and water it resembles the colour of a cuckoo, O foremost of the Bhṛgus;
in fermented gruel it is black, in kodo millet reddish-brown, / in buttermilk it is brownish-grey like honey, blue, or yellow;
in clarified butter it looks like water, in whey it has the colour of a pigeon, / in honey it is green, and in oil it is reddish;
unripe fruits ripen swiftly, / while ripe ones spoil, and garlands wither;

hard things become soft, and soft things the reverse; / fine threads stick together, and hairiness appears on them;
dark circular marks form, especially on clothes; / and metals and jewels are covered with dirt and slime;
ointments, perfumes, and bath-waters, O foremost of the Bhṛgus, / become foul-smelling, and leaves wither;
the collyrium, O Rāma, becomes yellow, blue, or white; / the bark of the tooth-cleaning stick ceases to yield, and strings appear [on it].
Such signs are to be recognised, O foremost of the Bhṛgus. / Therefore a king should always remain surrounded by gems, mantras, and herbs,
protected, O Rāma, by trustworthy men free from carelessness.
In this world the ruler of the earth is the root of the tree of subjects; through his protection the kingdom thrives. / Therefore the protection of the king must be undertaken with every effort by all, O moon of the Bhṛgu lineage.
Rāma said:
Expound the science of architecture, O son of the king of water creatures, / for the welfare of kings and of others, O bestower of honour.

Puṣkara said:
First one should inspect the land endowed with auspicious marks, / sloping towards the east and north, auspicious, and raised towards the south;
not cut through by gravel, not inundated by water from elsewhere, / free from anthills, mice burrows, chasms, and thorny trees;
soft to the touch, firm, not saline, / not shaped like barley, not resembling an elephant or a thunderbolt,
not shaped like a winnowing basket or a tortoise, not lacking vitality, / not infested with worms, possessing an abundance of soil, and auspicious;
in whose pit a flower does not wither, / and in which a lamp does not go out, O descendant of Bhṛgu;
and in which water is not quickly absorbed, O Rāma— / that earth is praiseworthy; upon it a wise man should establish a dwelling.
White, red, yellow, and black soil respectively / are praiseworthy for brahmins and the other [castes], O twice-born;

and having a smell like clarified butter, blood, grain, or liquor, / and having a sweet, astringent, sour, or pungent taste;
and covered with kuśa, śara, kāśa, or dūrvā grass. / Having carefully examined the land at an auspicious lunar day and asterism,
having first honoured the brahmins, one should clear it of bones and debris; / then, having excavated it, one should assign the portions of the deities.
Having first made the site sixty-four plots according to rule, / having divided it into sixty-four divisions on all sides,
one should assign each house there accordingly; / for the placement of doors is taught according to the division of the town.
Hereafter I shall declare the deities' portions according to rule: / In the centre, the four-plot master is Brahmā, the auspicious four-faced one; / to the east, the master is declared to be Aryaman, the restrainer; / to the south is Vivasvat, and Mitra stands to the west;

to the north is Pṛthvīdhara; now listen to me regarding the intermediate directions: / in the intermediate direction of Śiva, the two gods are Kāma and Pakṣa;
Sāvitrī and Savitṛ are declared in the southeast; / and in the southwest, O Rāma, Jaya and Indra are declared;
Bhadra and Vyādhi are declared in the northwest, O son of Bhṛgu. / Outside these deities, moreover, in the outer ring,
learn from me those deities in the directions, beginning with the east: / Mahendra, Ravi, Satya, and Bhṛśa are proclaimed in the east, O Rāma;
Gṛhakṣita, Yama, Bhṛṅga, and Gandharva in the south; / Bhallāṭa, Soma, Aditi, and Dhanada
[These are] traditionally taught as the deities on the north. Now listen to me [regarding those] in the intermediate corners: / Diti and Īśa, and Megha and Jaya, are proclaimed in the corner of Śiva.
Vyoman and Agni, and Pūṣan and Vitatha [are] in the corner of Agni, O descendant of Bhṛgu. / And in the southwest [are] Mṛga, Pitṛ, Īśa, Dauvārika, and Sugrīva.
Roga, Anāyu, Nāga, and Mukhya stand in the direction of the Wind. / Now listen to me as I declare the eight on the outside even of these:

One should make it there [where it does not] conflict with a solitary tree, O foremost of the twice-born. / Alternatively, house construction is undertaken according to the division of the directions:
the house for the deities in the northeast, and the kitchen in the southeast; / the fire-chamber there as well, and the drain in the southwest;
the storehouse and the armoury are recorded in the northwest. / Water from a well and the like is praised in the northern directions;
in the other [directions] it is censured, O Rāma, and one should avoid it with effort. / In a house, one should avoid timber that is old mixed with new.
One who knows should not build a shared wall or another's wall; / nor, except during an eclipse of the sun or moon, an alteration of the door.
One should not make an expansion or reduction when work on the house is resumed; / or they should be made on the northeast with the consent of brahmins. / The Brahma-place must always be made pure, O descendant of Bhṛgu;
and it should not be obstructed with pegs and the like. / One should know the vital points, O Rāma, at the junctions of the plots of the deities;
